Teaching point of this one (different from the other nine): BUFFER OWNERSHIP
WITH BORROWED REFERENCES. The reader gets a pointer into the producer's memory
and a refcount decides when the producer may reuse it.

------------------------------------------------------------------------------
Background

A Verkada camera SoC's capture unit hands you finished frames one at a time.
The stream has several consumers with very different speeds: the motion
detector wants the newest frame at full rate, the encoder is usually a frame
behind, and the cloud uploader stalls for tens of milliseconds whenever the
link hiccups. They all live in the same process.

The vendor gives you exactly one function (capture.h):

    #define FRAME_BYTES       4096      /* small on purpose; a real 4K frame
                                           is ~8 MB, which is the whole point */
    #define CAPTURE_PERIOD_US 25000     /* ~40 fps */

    int capture_next_frame(uint8_t *dst, uint64_t *timestamp);

  return 0   Blocked until the next frame was ready (~25 ms, with jitter), then
             copied FRAME_BYTES into dst and wrote the capture time into
             *timestamp (microseconds, capture_now_us() clock).
  return -1  Sensor glitch. Returns immediately, writes nothing. Happens
             occasionally and means "try again", not "give up".

  It keeps static state and is NOT thread-safe: exactly one thread may call it.
  While it is running, dst belongs to the sensor — it DMAs the frame in
  bursts, so anyone reading dst mid-call sees a half-written frame.

Blocking, single-caller, and it owns your buffer for 25 ms at a time. Wrap it
in something non-blocking and thread-safe that does not copy 8 MB per reader.

  Scaling note: unlike the original ALS problem there is nothing to shrink —
  25 ms is already short. What IS shrunk is Part 2's window: 2 seconds instead
  of the 10 s of pre-roll a real camera keeps, so the harness finishes in about
  4 seconds. Sizes and rules do not change, only the number.

------------------------------------------------------------------------------
Tasks

Part 1.  The latest frame, borrowed — not copied.

    const uint8_t *frame_acquire_latest(uint64_t *ts_out);
    void           frame_release(const uint8_t *frame);

  A reader must get the most recent COMPLETE frame with no copy of
  FRAME_BYTES, must never see a partially written frame, and must never wait
  on the sensor. The writer must never wait on a reader: if a reader sits on a
  frame for 100 ms, the writer keeps capturing and counts dropped frames.
  Pointers stay valid until the matching frame_release().

    uint64_t frame_dropped_count(void);   /* no free buffer -> frame discarded */
    uint64_t frame_release_errors(void);  /* foreign pointer / extra release   */
    size_t   frame_borrowed_count(void);  /* borrows outstanding right now     */

  A dropped frame is dropped completely: it does not appear in Part 2 either.

Part 2.  A short replay buffer (~2 seconds).

    int    frame_at(uint64_t t, uint8_t *dst, uint64_t *actual_ts);
    size_t frame_count_in(uint64_t t0, uint64_t t1);

  frame_at() copies out the frame in effect at time t — the newest frame whose
  timestamp <= t — and reports that frame's real timestamp. -1 if t is in the
  future, older than FRAME_REPLAY_WINDOW_US, or before the oldest frame held.
  frame_count_in() counts stored frames with t0 <= ts <= t1.

  Memory is NOT free here: FRAME_REPLAY_BYTES_MAX is a hard budget. Do the
  arithmetic out loud (frames/second x seconds x FRAME_BYTES), pick a capacity,
  and say what you evict when the budget is reached. Lookups must not be a
  linear scan.

    size_t frame_replay_bytes(void);      /* must never exceed the budget */

------------------------------------------------------------------------------
Expected behaviour  (what main.c checks, all against recorded ground truth)

  - A frame is available the moment frame_store_init() returns, with the
    timestamp the sensor reported, and two acquires of the same frame return
    the SAME pointer (borrowed, not copied).
  - 4 reader threads borrow frames for 2 s while the writer runs: every frame
    handed out must match its own sequence pattern (the fake sensor fills each
    frame from its sequence number and writes it in two bursts, so a torn
    frame is detectable), and no frame/timestamp may be invented.
  - The writer never blocks: with several distinct frames pinned by readers,
    the sensor keeps being drained and frame_dropped_count() grows instead.
    Pinned frames stay intact and nothing deadlocks.
  - Refcount hygiene: releasing twice, or releasing a pointer that was never
    handed out, is detected and does not corrupt the store; release(NULL) is a
    no-op; the outstanding-borrow count returns to 0.
  - Replay matches ground truth: exact timestamp match, a t between two frames
    returns the older one, t in the future -> -1, t older than the window
    -> -1, t before the first frame ever captured -> -1, and the frame
    covering the window edge is still there.
  - frame_count_in matches ground truth; reversed and future ranges give 0.
  - Replay memory stays inside the budget.
  - Both getters stay far under one vendor call (limit 5 ms vs 25 ms).
  - deinit() joins the writer thread promptly.

  Last line is "ALL CHECKS PASSED (0 failed)" or "N CHECK(S) FAILED".

