Things the interviewer is listening for   (answer on paper BEFORE coding)

  1. Why can a reader not just be handed a copy of the frame? Work out the
     cost: 4 consumers x 30 fps x 8 MB. What does that do to the memory bus?
  2. How many frame buffers do you need, and what does each extra one buy?
     Walk double -> triple -> N: what does the third buffer fix, what does it
     cost, and does it reduce latency or only absorb a stalled reader?
  3. What exactly makes it safe for the writer to reuse a buffer? Write the
     predicate. Why is "the newest frame" excluded even at refcount 0?
  4. What is inside your critical section? How long is it in nanoseconds? Where
     do the two 4 KiB copies in your design happen, and why not under the lock?
  5. The uploader stalls for 200 ms holding a frame. Walk through what the
     writer does on every frame during those 200 ms. Who decides to drop, and
     what does the reader that wakes up see? Why is blocking the writer wrong
     even though "it is only 200 ms"?
  6. Part 2 sizing: frames/second x window x FRAME_BYTES. What do you do when
     the answer is 640 MB? What is the worst-case frame rate, and what happens
     if the sensor bursts above nominal?
  7. Part 2 structure and costs: ring + binary search vs list vs balanced tree.
     What is O() for append, lookup at t, eviction, and count in a range? Why
     is a ring the right first answer here when it was only the first step in
     the ALS problem?
  8. Eviction: which frame do you drop, and which frame that is OLDER than the
     window must you keep? Why? And what happens if the slot you are about to
     recycle is being copied out by a reader right now?
  9. capture_next_frame() returns -1. What do you do, and why must you not
     just retry in a tight loop? What if the timestamps go backwards (NTP
     step) — what breaks, and how do you keep the binary search valid?
 10. Start-up and shutdown: what does a reader see before the first frame —
     NULL, or does init block ~25 ms to get one? What happens to a borrowed
     pointer when deinit() runs, and how long can join() take when the writer
     is parked inside the vendor call?
